---
title: Mashed Potatoes
source: direct-input
servings: 12
---

Peel @potatoes{3 1/4%pounds}(russet). Quarter them: Cut each potato in half, then cut each half in half again. If a potato tapers, cut it so the pieces are as even in size as possible. Place the cut pieces in a colander and rinse them very thoroughly under cold water to remove excess starch.

Transfer the rinsed potatoes to a #pot{}. Cover with cold water. Generously salt the water—this seasons the potatoes from the inside. Bring the pot to a boil over high heat, then reduce the heat to maintain a steady simmer. Cook until the potatoes are perfectly tender. Test by piercing a piece with a knife; it should slide in with almost no effort. Drain the potatoes very thoroughly in a colander, allowing all excess water to escape.

While the potatoes cook, cut @butter{1%pound}(unsalted) into chunks.

Return the well-drained, hot potatoes to the pot. Place the pot over medium-low heat to keep everything warm. Using a classic wire #potato masher{}, mash the potatoes thoroughly until no large lumps remain. (Don't worry about overmixing here.)

Add a few generous pinches of @salt{} and @ground black pepper{} to the mashed potatoes. Add about one-quarter of the butter chunks (about 1 stick). Mash with the potato masher until the butter is almost fully absorbed. Switch to a wire whisk. Add the second quarter of butter and whisk until it’s almost completely incorporated. Continue adding the butter in two more quarters, whisking thoroughly after each addition, until all the butter is used. The mixture will become incredibly light, creamy, and pale.

Pour in @milk{1/4%cup}(hot) and whisk one final time to combine and lighten the texture. Taste critically for seasoning. Add more salt if needed—this is crucial for the flavor. Add an optional shake of @?cayenne pepper{}.

Transfer to a serving bowl. Garnish only with @chives{} if taking a photo, otherwise serve plain. Serve immediately and enjoy the decadent, buttery perfection.